-0.03510735556483269, -0.0034985190723091364, -0.05632982403039932, 0.052258580923080444, 0.009000054560601711, -0.04007010906934738, -0.03662339597940445, -0.021950725466012955, 0.002402890007942915, 0.03427823260426521, 0.06537966430187225, 0.022750124335289, 0.0052199894562363625, 0.004943006206303835, 0.05026663839817047, -0.023162418976426125, 0.04168383404612541, 0.0052448417991399765, 0.011588898487389088, -0.03353654220700264], images:"none", previous_chunk_id:"ac168c8709ce3369fc7d22e3cf2cf1d6", has_images:false, header:"**7.2 KEPLER'S LAWS**", source:"processed_notes_with_descriptions.md", text:">\n> The planet P is shown in a position that is approximately halfway between the perihelion (A) and the aphelion (B). This position suggests that the planet's distance from the Sun at this time is equal to the semi-major axis of its elliptical orbit, as mentioned in the document.\n>\n> The diagram also includes a circular sector labeled \"D\", which appears to be swept out during a small interval of time (d*t), representing an area of space covered by the planet's path. This concept is related to the law of areas discussed in the document, where it mentions that for a planet, the line joining any planet to the Sun sweeps equal areas in equal intervals of time.\n>", chunk_id:"a6e442af46219986ffde8de6b765e249", next_chunk_id:"b035f6f35f5275a98497377a015a5616"}}, {_id:784, properties:{text_embedding:[-0.02253764308989048, -0.026386968791484833, -0.06356536597013474, 0.0021705045364797115, -0.035508327186107635, -0.06139950454235077, -0.015509549528360367, -0.008909455500543118, 0.011533415876328945, 0.0042236438021063805, -0.014820529147982597, -9.311860776506364E-4, 0.008509103208780289, 0.010227364487946033, 0.01923890970647335, 0.007556629832834005, 0.024355212226510048, -0.013639449141919613, 0.02106049656867981, -0.0037951390258967876, -0.0030802099499851465, 9.012057562358677E-4, 0.004805461503565311, 0.03450922295451164, -0.018264783546328545, 0.00725428154692