ording to result stated in the last section, they exert no gravitational force on mass *m* kept at P. The shells with radius ≤ *r* make up a sphere of radius r for which the point P lies on the surface. This smaller sphere therefore exerts a force on a mass *m* at P as if its mass *Mr* is concentrated at the centre. Thus the force on the mass *m* at P has a magnitude  \n$$F = \\frac{Gm (M_r)}{r^2} \\quad (7.9)$$  \nWe assume that the entire earth is of uniform  \ndensity and hence its mass is <sup>3</sup> E 4 3 *MRE* <sup>π</sup> = <sup>ρ</sup> where *ME* is the mass of the earth *RE* is its radius and ρ is the density. On the other hand the mass of the sphere *Mr* of radius *r* is 4 <sup>3</sup> 3 *r* π ρ and hence", chunk_id:"0d5ccbffbde914b853d1ad4489973834", next_chunk_id:"ee437284e60407b543b0dbd31d016097"}}, {_id:815, properties:{text_embedding:[-0.009318460710346699, -0.004100179765373468, 0.007519968785345554, -0.03415394201874733, 0.004752404987812042, -0.07203203439712524, 0.01656033657491207, 0.038817308843135834, 0.010668950155377388, 0.014271169900894165, -0.06555525958538055, 7.296537514775991E-4, -0.021087639033794403, 0.014524050988256931, 0.027924243360757828, 0.010594743303954601, -0.010295944288372993, -0.002510357415303588, 0.012302514165639877, -0.007238013204187155, -0.012301516719162464, 0.01803288236260414, 0.017776325345039368, 0.009019311517477036, 0.012565605342388153, 0.04765015468001366, 0.0032900338992476463, -7.964673568494618E-4, -0.004152785521000624, 0.017355596646666527, 0.01489591971039772, -0.008125104010105133, -8.289305260404944E-4, -0.024286340922117233, -0.05564318597316742, 0.039324626326560974, -0.015520431101322174, -0.002653151750564575, -0.05516683682799339, -0.02855408936738968, -0.03046693094074726, 0.005455792415887117, 0.04269383102655411, -0.021289190277457237, 0.0144659373909235, -0.0469859279692173, -0.041951872408390045, -0.0662168636918068, -0.027579080313444138, -0.014615457504987717, -0.016628960147500038, 0.00