7.27)$$  \n*Fig. 7.9*  \nBy the principle of energy conservation Eqs. (7.26) and (7.27) must be equal. Hence  \n$$\\frac{mV_i^2}{2} - \\frac{GmM_E}{(h + R_E)} = \\frac{mV_f^2}{2} \\quad (7.28)$$  \nThe R.H.S. is a positive quantity with a minimum value zero hence so must be the L.H.S. Thus, an object can reach infinity as long as V*<sup>i</sup>* is such that  \n$$\\frac{mV_i^2}{2} - \\frac{GmM_E}{(h + R_E)} \\geq 0 \\quad (7.29)$$  \nThe minimum value of V*<sup>i</sup>* corresponds to the case when the L.H.S. of Eq. (7.29) equals zero. Thus, the minimum speed required for an object to reach infinity (i.e. escape from the earth) corresponds to  \n$$\\frac{1}{2} m (V_t^2)_{\\min} = \\frac{GmM_E}{h + R_E} \\quad (7.30)$$  \nIf the object is thrown from the surface of the earth, *h* = 0, and we get  \n$$(V_i)_{\\min} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM_E}{R_E}} \\quad (7.31)$$  \nUsing the relation <sup>2</sup> *g GM R* = *EE* / , we get", chunk_id:"86bde5ad907e07316de544f7da4ec517", next_chunk_id:"4cb36fdc1910f0d6bcb755d1548c644a"}}, {_id:837, properties:{text_embedding:[-0.01085737720131874, 0.020326796919107437, -0.033707357943058014, -0.025036225095391273, -0.012760278768837452, -0.035779088735580444, -0.04369671642780304, 0.014159663580358028, -0.026667721569538116, -0.008613471873104572, -0.07187146693468094, -0.004733008798211813, -0.03796381503343582, 0.01706847734749317, 0.018626619130373, 0.02453158050775528, -0.010078811086714268, 0.029079055413603783, 0.014272267930209637, 0.046315018087625504, 0.030708858743309975, 0.07473379373550415, 0.021173950284719467, 0.0034232609905302525, 0.056046999990940094, 0.03187447786331177, -0.025765148922801018, -0.02173701673746109, 0.010979149490594864, -9.888248750939965E-4, -0.02363749034702778, 0.03667590022087097, -0.059914954006671906, -0.05033831670880318, -0.041336581110954285, 0.04646875709295273, -0.03700557351112366, 0.0062059061601758, -0.02704184502363205, 0.023832542821764946, -0.025414323434233665, -0.04105643555521965, 0.02